      "recipeIngredient": [
        "2 tablespoons extra-virgin olive oil, plus more for serving (optional)",
        "½ cup finely chopped carrot (from 1 medium carrot)",
        "Salt and black pepper",
        "5 garlic cloves, minced, divided",
        "2 tablespoons tomato paste",
        "¾ cup farro",
        "4 cups low-sodium chicken broth",
        "1 large egg",
        "½ cup plain panko bread crumbs",
        "¾ cup freshly grated Parmesan, divided, plus more for serving",
        "¼ cup chopped parsley, divided",
        "1 teaspoon dried oregano",
        "½ teaspoon red pepper flakes",
        "1 pound ground chicken, preferably dark meat",
        "1 cup frozen peas"
      ],
      "recipeInstructions": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "In a large Dutch oven, heat 2 tablespoons oil over medium. Add carrot, season with salt and pepper and cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, 3 minutes. Add about a third of the minced garlic and stir until fragrant, 30 seconds. Add tomato paste and cook, stirring, 1 minute. Add farro and broth, season with salt and pepper and bring to a boil over medium-high heat. Cover, reduce heat to medium-low and simmer for 15 minutes.",
          "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/791401712-farro-stew-chicken-meatballs-peas#recipe-step-1"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Meanwhile, in a medium bowl, combine egg, panko, ¼ cup of the Parmesan, 2 tablespoons of the parsley, oregano, red pepper flakes and the remaining garlic and mix well. Add chicken, ½ teaspoon salt and ½ teaspoon pepper and mix just until well incorporated. Form into 12 equal meatballs.",
          "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/791401712-farro-stew-chicken-meatballs-peas#recipe-step-2"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add meatballs to the pot with the farro mixture (they will not be completely submerged) and bring broth to a simmer over medium-high heat. Cover, reduce heat to medium-low and cook for 5 minutes. Uncover and cook, stirring occasionally, until meatballs are cooked through and farro is tender but toothsome, adding peas halfway through, about 5 minutes longer. Turn off heat and stir in the remaining ½ cup Parmesan and 2 tablespoons parsley.",
          "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/791401712-farro-stew-chicken-meatballs-peas#recipe-step-3"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Divide stew among bowls. Drizzle with olive oil, if using, and top with more Parmesan and black pepper. Serve warm.",
          "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/791401712-farro-stew-chicken-meatballs-peas#recipe-step-4"
        }
      ],
      "recipeYield": "4 servings",
      "review": [
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": "David"
          },
          "datePublished": "2026-10-13T09:42:14Z",
          "reviewBody": "Very good stew for the cool fall nights! I substituted dark turkey as I couldn't find dark chicken meat. \n\nI seared the meat balls first for added flavor then sautéed the carrots and garlic in the leftover fat. I doubled/tripled the garlic (as one does) and parmesan cheese. Came out very garlickly and delicious.\n\nWould definitely make again!"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": "Miles"
          },
          "datePublished": "2026-10-12T22:35:37Z",
          "reviewBody": "Made as written and absolutely delicious. Into the weeknight rotation it goes!"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": "TNC"
          },
          "datePublished": "2026-10-12T16:19:27Z",
          "reviewBody": "I’ve been making this for years… I use trader joes precooked chicken meatballs and 10 min farro if I’m in a hurry on a weeknight!"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": "Alex"
          },
          "datePublished": "2026-10-10T16:07:54Z",
          "reviewBody": "Made this last night and it was delicious - swapped the farro out for pearl barley (much easier to get hold of in the UK - hadn't even heard of farro before!) and used turkey mince instead, otherwise followed as written. Feels like you could mess about it with a fair bit too, will definitely make again soon."
        },
